# 11  Conclusion

Our primary contribution is the cross-vendor characterization of WebGPU dispatch overhead: sequential-dispatch measurement reveals true per-dispatch cost (24–36 μs Vulkan, 31–71 μs Metal)—20× lower than naive single-op benchmarks—validated across four GPU vendors, three backends, three browsers, and four architectures. This finding is dtype-independent and API-inherent. We distinguish per-dispatch cost (WebGPU API alone) from per-operation overhead (~95 μs including Python/framework), with the latter being stack-specific. The fusion experiment provides the cleanest causal evidence: reducing 312 dispatches with unchanged kernel quality yields 53% end-to-end improvement, confirming per-operation overhead as the actionable bottleneck at batch=1. Backend choice (Vulkan vs Metal) is the dominant factor for optimization behavior, though implementation choice also matters within a backend (2.2× for Metal). End-to-end inference spans three platforms with CUDA, MPS, CPU, and browser baselines (Table 3). The torch-webgpu results (21.0/17.9 tok/s, RTX 5090/Dawn) provide illustrative context for one configuration.

